[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Custom Renderers and Effects Architecture in Xamarin"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Xamarin.Forms provides a cross-platform UI abstraction, but there are times when you need to access native platform-specific controls. Custom Renderers and Effects are the architectural pillars designed to bridge this gap, allowing developers to customize or extend the appearance and behavior of native controls without losing shared code efficiency."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The choice between a Custom Renderer and an Effect depends heavily on your use case. Custom Renderers are best when you want to completely replace or heavily modify the lifecycle and layout of a control. Effects, on the other hand, are lightweight and designed for styling adjustments and minor native event handling without subclassing."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Creating a Custom Effect in Shared Code"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, create a RoutingEffect subclass in your shared project. This acts as the bridge that platform-specific implementations will target."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"using Xamarin.Forms;

namespace AppNamespace
{
    public class FocusEffect : RoutingEffect
    {
        public FocusEffect() : base("AppNamespace.FocusEffect")
        {
        }
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Implementing the Effect on Android"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, implement the platform-specific renderer for Android by overriding the OnAttached and OnDetached methods."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"using Android.Graphics.Drawables;
using Android.Widget;
using Xamarin.Forms;
using Xamarin.Forms.Platform.Android;

[assembly: ResolutionGroupName("AppNamespace")]
[assembly: ExportEffect(typeof(AppNamespace.Droid.FocusEffectImplementation), "FocusEffect")]
namespace AppNamespace.Droid
{
    public class FocusEffectImplementation : PlatformEffect
    {
        protected override void OnAttached()
        {
            try
            {
                if (Control is EditText editText)
                {
                    editText.SetBackgroundColor(global::Android.Graphics.Color.LightGreen);
                }
            }
            catch (System.Exception ex)
            {
                System.Diagnostics.Debug.WriteLine("Cannot set property on attached control: {0}", ex.Message);
            }
        }

        protected override void OnDetached()
        {
            // Clean up resources if needed
        }
    } 
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Consuming the Effect in XAML"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Finally, attach your newly created effect to any standard Xamarin.Forms control via XAML using the Effects collection property."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"<ContentPage xmlns="http://xamarin.com/schemas/2014/forms"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             xmlns:local="clr-namespace:AppNamespace"
             x:Class="AppNamespace.MainPage">
    <StackLayout Padding="20">
        <Entry Placeholder="Focused Entry">
            <Entry.Effects>
                <local:FocusEffect />
            </Entry.Effects>
        </Entry>
    </StackLayout>
</ContentPage>"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer Effects over Custom Renderers whenever you only need to modify styling properties, as they can be reused across different control types."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always handle memory cleanup inside OnDetached methods to avoid memory leaks on native platforms."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure resolution group names and effect names match precisely across your shared and platform-specific projects."}]
